From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 09:36:33 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Embedding: deliver unmasked x87 exceptions

x86 raises #MF at the next waiting x87 or MMX instruction after an x87
instruction raises an exception the control word unmasks, and the
raising instruction commits less than its masked form: after invalid
operation, a denormal operand or division by zero nothing but the
status word, and a store beside an overflow or underflow writes
nothing. An embedding host that runs x86 programs exactly needs both.

The floating-point mode an embedder sets now also says whether the x87
control word unmasks an exception, and FLDCW, FLDENV, FRSTOR and FNINIT
-- so FNSAVE -- leave a context whose mode they change. In that mode:

- every waiting instruction starts with an EmbedderX87Wait op, which
  leaves with EMBEDDER_EXIT_X87_FAULT while ES shows an exception
  pending, before the instruction does anything;
- the helpers, the inline exception paths, the stack faults and the
  comparisons record the unmasked exceptions the running instruction
  raised, and EmbedderX87Commit ops after each lowered operation, before
  a store and before a comparison's pop leave with
  EMBEDDER_EXIT_X87_EXCEPTION, the instruction retired with only its
  status word changed; a comparison sets its condition codes first, as
  an Intel host does, and a load completes beside a denormal operand;
- FLDCW makes ES and B show whether the new control word unmasks a flag
  already set.

Embedded, FLDENV, FRSTOR, FXRSTOR and XRSTOR set ES and B from the
flags and the control word they load. A helper that follows an
unmasked invalid operation, denormal operand or division by zero of
its instruction raises none of a result's exceptions, and a store
conversion beside an unmasked overflow or underflow raises no
precision.
